  "notes_context": "These notes were distilled from earlier work in this repository that passed verification. Follow them unless the code or CHANGELOG.md shows they no longer hold.\n- [at-0007] Refunds above 500.00 need approved_by: Per finance policy FIN-2026-03, support-initiated refunds and credits above 500.00 require a second approver: accept keyword-only `approved_by=None` and raise `BillingError(\"approval_required\")` when the amount exceeds the threshold and no approver is given. Amounts at or below 500.00 need no approval.\n  (learned from task E04 on ledgerly 1.0.0; source FIN-2026-03; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n  WARNING: learned on version 1.0.0, this checkout is 2.0.0. Check CHANGELOG.md before relying on it.\n- [at-0008] Record state changes via audit.record: Every payment state transition must be logged with `ledgerly.audit.record` using a snake_case event name and the payment id and amount.\n  (learned from task E06 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n  WARNING: learned on version 1.0.0, this checkout is 2.0.0. Check CHANGELOG.md before relying on it.\n- [at-0003] Raise BillingError with stable snake_case codes: Reject invalid billing input by raising `BillingError(code, detail)` from `ledgerly.errors` with codes like `invalid_state`, `invalid_quantity`, `negative_amount`, matching the existing `issue_invoice` pattern.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 3x, 3 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0006] Money operations take keyword-only idempotency_key: Every function that moves money or changes a payment, credit or invoice balance must accept keyword-only `idempotency_key=None` and check it first, before state validation: if `ledgerly.audit.events()` already has an entry with that key, return the original record without reapplying. Pass `idempotency_key` into the `audit.record(...)` call.\n  (learned from task E04 on ledgerly 1.0.0; source INC-204; relied on 1x, 1 passed, 0 failed)\n  WARNING: learned on version 1.0.0, this checkout is 2.0.0. Check CHANGELOG.md before relying on it.\n- [at-0001] Round money only with round_money: Always round monetary Decimal results with ledgerly.money.round_money (half-up to the cent); never use Python's round() or a bare Decimal.quantize(), which use banker's rounding and produce wrong invoice totals.\n  (learned from task E01 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 4x, 3 passed, 1 failed)\n- [at-0002] Normalize money via to_money and round_money: Convert any monetary input with `to_money` from `ledgerly.money` and store it rounded with `round_money`; compare against `ZERO` rather than a literal Decimal.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 5x, 4 passed, 0 failed)",
